Sure! To do that, you’ll need to pass your array of objects to the `prioritizeAndSort` function. Here’s how you can call it:

```javascript
const result = prioritizeAndSort({
  items: [
    { name: "Alpha", status: "normal" },
    { name: "Beta", status: "urgent" },
    { name: "Gamma", status: "normal" }
  ],
  priorityStatus: "urgent",
  ascending: true
});
```

Replace the sample array with your own `myItemList`. The function will return a new array where all items with `status === "urgent"` appear first, followed by the rest sorted alphabetically by `name` in ascending order.
